  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1120 AM CDT Sat Aug 22 2026\n\nToday and Tonight...\n\nWater vapor imagery and objective analysis late this morning depict \nan amplified ridge in place over the western CONUS with \nsubsequent troughing in place over the eastern CONUS. Sandwiched\nbetween the two, we continued to reside under northwesterly \nflow aloft as a train of disturbance slide over the ridge and \ninto the area. Surface high pressure is filtering into the area \nfollowing the departure of morning showers and storms, helping \nto bring a dry and pleasant afternoon with plenty of sunshine. \nHigh temperatures are expected to peak in the mid 80s, near \nseasonal norms for mid-August. \n\nPatchy fog development is expected overnight, especially over far \neastern Nebraska and southwest Iowa where clear skies will bring \nradiational cooling and winds remain the lightest. Model soundings \nindicate a narrow saturated layer near the surface, likely \nconfining fog to low-lying and wind protected areas.\n\nSunday...\n\nLate tonight into Sunday morning, a round of convection is expected \nto initiate east of the Front Range as a shortwave disturbance \npivots into the area. Into Sunday morning, the convection is \nexpected to progress eastward across Nebraska, bringing PoPs of\n40-60% into east-central Nebraska, generally in the 4-9 AM \ntimeframe. Instability will gradually decrease as storm progress\ntowards the area, limiting overall storm intensity and \ncoverage with eastern progression. Lingering cloud cover, and \nperhaps a shower or two lingering into the afternoon, will keep \nhigh temperatures confined to the upper 70s and low 80s.\n\nLater Sunday, convection is expected to initiate across east-central \nSouth Dakota as an midlevel shortwave disturbance pivots into the \nnorthern Plains. Convection is expected to becoming more widespread \ninto the late evening/early overnight period of the LLJ ramps up, \nhelping to form a larger cluster of thunderstorms that will \neventually push southeastward, into eastern Nebraska and western \nIowa. How far south this complex progresses into the area remains in \nquestion, as instability remains maximized around 1500 J/kg of\nMLCAPE in northeast Nebraska, decreasing southward. However, increasing\nlow- level moisture transport and WAA could help support \nscattered thunderstorms through the overnight period. For now, \nPoPs peak in the 50-75% range north of Interstate-80. A strong\nto severe storm can't be ruled out, though the limited\ninstability over much of the area should help to limit any \nwidespread severe weather. \n\nMonday and Beyond...\n\nInto the work week, we will continue in the pattern or northwesterly \nflow aloft with a train of disturbances bringing periodic \nprecipitation chances. High temperatures will generally reside in \nthe low to mid 80s through the week. Tuesday night into early \nWednesday, a more potent disturbance pushing across the central and \nnorthern Plains will push a cold front southward across the area. \nThe front won't have much of an impact on temperatures, but will \nbring a round of 30-50% PoPs to the area. Periodic 15% PoPs \nexist through the week, though dry periods are expected to \noutweigh wet ones.",
